#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180496} :activity/id #uuid "3e766c6c-8044-509d-9e8e-e4e4bbddcfe5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179602 :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259843}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "3bb7385f-5c70-5eed-b8c4-e230a20a67a7" :step/math-academy-id 35728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179603 :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369291}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"} :step/id #uuid "8aff9a77-6b15-506d-8bc4-aca89e0ff920" :step/math-academy-id 35730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180719 :tutorial/content "Given a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and a path $C$ defined by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ the line integral \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\ngives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from the initial point $\\mathbf{r}(a)$ to the terminal point $\\mathbf{r}(b)$ on the curve.\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a *closed* curve. The **circulation** of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C,$ denoted using the Greek letter $\\Gamma$ (pronounced \"gamma\"), is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nRemember that the notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ simply reminds us that we're integrating along a closed path.\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ gives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along the entire closed path. We can also think of $\\Gamma$ as a measure of how much $\\mathbf F$ circulates around $C.$\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering some examples. \n\n- Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction, shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e9de4e3734be4f7d8f59f6d54d0ed92c51db3a0beb8277f4b450531b7dab332.png)\n\nIt's clear from the picture that $\\mathbf{F}$ *assists* the motion of a particle as it moves counterclockwise along the path. Therefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >    0.\n$$\n\n- Now imagine that $C$ is traversed in the clockwise direction.\n\n![](images/16/16dd701d51e3b43714b58993f66e42359289f557ef86fd9091ab6b8bba7041e7.png)\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf F$ works *against* the particle's motion as it moves clockwise along the path. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the vector field $\\mathbf{G}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the clockwise direction, shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf269cecceeefc3e81b868a4fc4c54127f00432145782b24719bb49552868c7.png)\n\nSince the force $\\mathbf G$ acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves around the path, it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf G\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4446c197-397c-5e41-a7a9-22d57126ffae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5833 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2145b39b-47ee-5ae2-ad16-55a2719cc2e0" :step/math-academy-id 35732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179605 :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369312}] :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "3bb98ffd-856f-5d48-bd6b-55f3885cdca8" :step/math-academy-id 35749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180721 :tutorial/content "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nNow, if $C$ is a closed path that's traversed once, then $\\mathbf r(a) = \\mathbf r(b),$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circulation of a conservative vector field around *any* closed path equals zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bc343b6-5187-5353-bb7b-23db12eaa417" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5836 :tutorial/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "345a9b9c-ae65-5c52-85e2-8ffa7905b813" :step/math-academy-id 35756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180499}}] :activity/title "Circulation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179865}]}
